## Who does what
Every reply of the main session re-reads its whole context, so the main session
should be a mid-price model that delegates reading and saves Opus for planning.

| Job | Who | Model |
|---|---|---|
| Plan a new app, page or UI (design and copy), multi-file work, unclear fixes | `planner` | Opus |
| Find code, read 3+ files, audits | `explorer` | Haiku |
| Run tests and commands | `runner` | Haiku |
| Small edits in files you already know | main session | (Sonnet) |
| Root cause of a failure | `debugger` | Sonnet |
| Web and docs lookups | `researcher` | Sonnet |
| Review a diff over ~30 lines or touching security, data or money | `reviewer` | Haiku |
| Costly-to-reverse decision, or two failed attempts | `big-decision` | Fable |

If the main session is running on Opus, it does no hands-on work at all: every read,
edit and command goes to a helper (`builder` for edits).

Building something new that people will see (an app, page, UI or marketing copy)? Ask
`planner` for the design first, then build exactly to it.

Give helpers precise tasks with paths. Independent helper calls go in one message.
If a helper is refused permission (for example, `runner` can't run a command), do that
step yourself instead of stopping; never end a task just because a helper was blocked.

## Every change
- Do only what was asked, in the fewest lines that fully solve it.
- Before adding a file, function or dependency, check one doesn't already exist.
- After a change, run the narrowest test that proves it. Two failed fixes → `debugger`.
- Unfamiliar area: read `CODEMAP.md` first. Run `/map` at the end only if files were
  added, moved or deleted.
- Replies: the answer, then at most one short follow-up line.
- Same correction twice, or "always/never": add a one-line rule to `.claude/LEARNED.md`.
- Never read or print secrets. Never commit `.env`, keys or tokens.

